# Derived audio — lives in share/ (audio/, stems/, ab/, the .eleven-ran sentinel), never
# committed. The directory entry is what covers backing tracks (.flac) and the sentinel;
# the extension rules catch stray renders written outside it.
share/
*.mp3
*.wav

# Python / tooling caches
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
*.pyc

# Every session, whole. One directory per run of the pipeline — the filled-in input, the
# week's diff, the ACT read, the letter — so this single rule covers all four stages, where
# it used to take four rules across four directories and still let a stray file through.
# These are the most personal files the repo touches, and an unattended run must never be
# committable by accident. Set PETROGRAPH_OUT to relocate the whole set somewhere backed up;
# the diffs in particular are a unique record, since File Recovery prunes at ~7 days.
#
# The templates stay tracked — they are the form, not the content. The re-include is
# explicit because git will not resurrect a file from inside an excluded directory, and the
# glob covers every TEMPLATE*.md variant, so adding one does not silently leave it untracked.
sessions/*
!sessions/TEMPLATE*.md

# NER source corpora — megabytes of raw note text, regenerable from the vault. Unlike
# reviews/, nothing here is a unique historical record, so none of it is worth committing.
corpus/

# Message exports — real message text from ~/Library/Messages/chat.db, and other
# people's words rather than my own. Regenerable from the database on demand, and
# strictly more sensitive than the note corpus: never committed, never published.
messages/

# Browsing history — what was open in Chrome, Firefox or Safari over the window, hour by
# hour, straight out of the browser's own database. Regenerable on demand, and about as
# revealing as anything the repo touches: grouping the visits makes the file shorter, not
# less personal, since the page titles are still in it. Never committed, never published to
# the gist. browsing_history.py refuses to write outside a gitignored path, so this entry
# is what makes the tool run at all.
browsing/

# Context attachments written by providers that had nowhere better to put them. Anything
# genuinely sensitive should be written into the repository that owns it instead — see
# providers.example.conf — but a provider with no `out` lands here, and here is ignored.
context/

# Provider configuration — the commands that assemble a week's context. Gitignored because
# it holds the paths of private repositories; providers.example.conf is the tracked copy.
providers.conf

# opencode's project config — the providers and models `--llm opencode` can reach. Gitignored
# because it holds the host names and keys of whatever you are pointing it at, which are as
# personal as a repository path; opencode.example.json is the tracked copy. opencode reads it
# from the directory the CLI ran in, and the run is made from the repository root, so this is
# where it belongs.
opencode.json

# The BAML client, generated from baml_src/ by `just baml`. 250-odd files and ~10 MB of
# emitted Python and vendored stdlib: regenerable, not source. The pairing that produced it is
# what is worth committing, and that lives in baml.toml and in the tool's own dependency pin.
tools/baml_sdk/
.baml/

# Secrets — API keys live here, never committed
.env

# Installed agent skills (npx skills add …) — regenerable, not source
.agents/
.claude/skills/
skills-lock.json

# OS
.DS_Store

# The one-off host. `site/public/` is assembled from synthetic/ by `just site-build` and is a copy
# of files already tracked there; node_modules is the Access plugin; .dev.vars and .wrangler hold
# the password and wrangler's own state. The gate in site/functions/ IS tracked — it is the thing
# that makes the deployment safe, and it fails closed, so it is worth reading in a diff.
site/public/
site/node_modules/
site/.dev.vars
# wrangler's state, wherever it is run from — the root or site/.
.wrangler/
# One real page, staged for the password-gated deployment. Never committed: it is the whole
# connectome of a private vault in a single file.
site/private/
# Names of every page ever published behind the password — see LEDGER in tools/site_private.py.
# Only filenames, but local state about a private deployment, so it stays local.
site/.private-published

# The opencode exemplar — a real read of a real week, kept next to the code because that is
# where weekly_review.py looks for it, and never committed: it is a whole week of one person,
# medications and other people included. The repo ships without it and the run skips it when it
# is missing, so supply your own if you want a local model held to a standard.
tools/example.md

# The real vault measured — about sixty numbers describing how the owner writes: edits per week,
# note and hunk lengths, typo and entity rates. No note text and no titles, but it is genuine
# behavioural data about a private practice rather than anything synthetic, so it stays local.
# `just synth-profile` rebuilds it from the vault; the generators exit with that instruction when
# it is missing, so a clone without a vault fails clean rather than silently inventing a shape.
synthetic/profile.json

# The same measurement as a prose brief: rates for edits per week, note and hunk lengths,
# filename shape, typo and name density, written from profile.json by `just synth-profile`. It is
# the description of a private notebook, not a description of a synthetic one, so it stays local
# with the profile it came from. The generators name it when it is missing.
synthetic/style-guide.md
